Reflectivity/Emissivity Files in MODTRAN

 
	In subdirectory 'Documentation.dir' the 'm3.5-upgrade.*' files itemize the 
upgrades between MODTRAN 3 and MODTRAN 3.5 and 'm3.0-upgrade' itemizes the 
upgrades between MODTRAN 2 and MODTRAN 3.0. 

        A compressed version of MODTRAN 3.5 is in the directory
'mod3.5-compress*' parallel to the uncompressed 'modtran3.5' directory. The
directory 'mod3.5-compress*' has two files, 'uncompress-instructions' and
'modtran3.5-v*.*.tar.gz' which has the entire directory compressed with the
gnu free software 'gzip'. Binary mode of ftp needs to be used when
getting the compressed file 'modtran3.5-v*.*.tar.gz'.


		MAKING THE BAND MODEL FILE 'MOLBMP96.BIN' 

	The two files, 'molbmp.f' and 'MOLBMP96.ASC', in subdirectory 'DATA' are 
needed for making the binary band model file 'MOLBMP96.BIN'. The file 
'molbmp.f' is compiled independently of the other code, and the executable 
should be run once to produce the 'MOLBMP96.BIN' file before the main 
MODTRAN 3.5 code is compiled. 'molbmp.f' tests for the minimum record length 
size, then states the correct number for 'RECL' to be used in 
'Source.dir/SOURCE/bmdata.f' where '"MOLBMP96.BIN' is opened to be read by the 
main MODTRAN 3.5 code.

	'molbmp.f' prompts the user for the names of the files to be read and 
written. The binary file name, 'MOLBMP96.BIN', is imbedded in the MODTRAN 3.5 
source file in 'bmdata.f', but can be changed. 'molbmp.f' offers the option 
of reading binary and writing ascii as well as the standard reading ascii and 
writing binary. 

	'molbmp.out' is an example of the output information that can be 
compared with the with the output produced when making 'MOLBMP96.BIN'. No 
compiler flags are needed. The old ascii band model tape UFTAPE.ASC is not 
compatible with MODTRAN 3.5. More information about the band model changes are 
listed in 'Documentation.dir/m3.5-upgrade.*'.


		COMPILING THE CODE & MACHINE SPECIFIC PARAMETERS

	The 'makefile' to be used with the UNIX 'make' utility is structured to be 
run in a directory with two subdirectories, 'SOURCE' and 'OBJECTS'. The 
subdirectory 'SOURCE' contains each subroutine as an independent file. The 
'makefile' puts the object files in subdirectory 'OBJECTS'. Local variables 
no longer need to be saved when compiling by using the machine dependent save 
option. The only compile options used are '-O' to optimize and '-c' to 
suppress linking and produce an object file from each source file.

	The subroutine loop.f does not have the optimization flag in the makefile. 
The solar single scattering calculation is slightly off when the Hp optimizes loop.f,
but the Sun and SGI optimizers are fine. If you choose to optimize loop.f ,check 
the solar single scattering numbers in tape6 for case1 against the numbers in case1.tp6.

	On the SGI subroutine driver.f the '-O' flag gives the warning:

uopt: Warning: driver_: this procedure not optimized because it
      exceeds size threshold; to optimize this procedure, use -Olimit option
      with value >= 1209.

This is not a problem in that the code compiles and runs; driver.f is simply not 
optimized. driver.f can be optimized by changing the '-O' flag to '-Olimit 1209'. 

	There are a couple of machine dependent parameters in the code.  
The file 'd1mach.f' contains double precision machine constants used 
in the DISORT multiple scattering section. The comments in 'd1mach.f' 
explain what appropriate changes need to be made.

	The record length, 'RECL', used for reading and writing the binary band 
model file 'MOLBMP96.BIN' is machine dependent and  needs to be put into
the open statement for 'MOLBMP96.BIN' in 'Source.dir/SOURCE/bmdata.f'. The 
correct number for the record length size, 'RECL', is determined when the 
binary file 'MOLBMP96.BIN' is written by 'molbmp.f' in subdirectory 'DATA'. RECL is 60 
for the Hp and Sun and is 15 for the SGI and DAC Alpha. It is important to make the 
binary band model tape before compiling the main MODTRAN 3.5 executable.

	The file 'PARAM.LST' contains the list of parameters used 
to change the number of layers. This file is incorporated into the 
MODTRAN 3.5 code with "include" statements.

	The files that are compiled and linked together in 'Source.dir/SOURCE' for 
the MODTRAN 3.5 executable are:

